what you're allowed to do but  also when it comes to the  kind of political goals that they gave  and people on the market for example the  government-sponsored enterprises like  Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac who had this  whole mission to make sure that more  mortgages went to people who could not  afford a mortgage on on the free market  and Bush increased that a lot and  increased the proportion of loans that  were supposed to go to people with low  income from 50% to 56% the number of  loans that were supposed to go to people  with a very low income increased from 20  to 28 percent and very low income means  you will not afford this house in the  future you previously mentioned the  federal reserve for a lot of people this  is a difficult concept to understand  myself included so you you basically  have the Federal Reserve pumping up this  bubble explain to us how this happened  why this happened this happened in a  situation that was very similar to where  we are today people are afraid of a an  economic crisis a bubble has just been  deflated at that time it was the dot-com  come bubble and we have been 9/11  terrorist terrorist attacks in 2001 and  then everybody said well what they're  saying today feds gotta save us somehow  they gotta make sure that there's a lot  of easy money in the economy so what  they did is that they lowered interest  rates dramatically made it possible for  people to borrow at a much cheaper costs  and lowering the interest rate from 6.75  to 1.75 percent in one year and  continued to reduce the interest rate  until it was down to one percent and  this basically means especially if  you've got a little implement inflation  that it's almost a negative interest  rate you basically make money by  borrowing from others it becomes  expensive to hold capital you won't get  any return yourself so instead you need  more leverage borrowing from others this  affected financial markets a lot because  then you try to find riskier asset  classes and you try to borrow more to  invest in them to get a return but it  also affects households because you can  suddenly see that oh my mortgage is much  less expensive if I get an adjustable  rate means that it feels like I'm  getting a house for free as someone put  it when they you tell you tell a story  in the book of somebody in Atlanta  correct who says I feel like I'm getting  a house for free and actually ends up  getting a third property on an  interest-only mortgage this as as you  point out was was driven not only to  save mark ISM after after the talk combo  but there's political interference here  tell us a little about how politics  played in this obsession with home  ownership well well it's an American  legend a myth almost the American dream  of having a home of your own and it's a  good one and as a Swede I really admire  that part of the American Dream but the  whole idea from the beginning was that  you are working hard to to earn this to  make sure that you earn your own home  but this suddenly changed with a  political idea of making sure that  people get a home first and then we'll  try to get well people can even  refinance by getting a second mortgage  because the price of the houses has  increased a lot but we've got them this  battery of policies that were aimed at  making sure that more people enter the  housing market we've got deductions of  the the interest rates we've got  government insurances to make sure that  they'll pay if you cannot pay we've got  some pressure on private lenders to make  sure that they lend more to people with  the low income but specifically we've  got the government's been sitting  sponsored enterprises Fannie Mae and  Freddie Mac the whole point of which was  to make sure that more people enter the  market more people got a mortgage and  these were not small corporations they  were some of the world's largest you in  your book say this isn't primarily a  bipartisan thing this is something that  that everyone is trying to benefit from  politically is that to give us an idea  of like you know who is fundamentally  responsible is it a both party thing is  that actually right yeah if this is a  crime story it turns out in the end that  everybody did it everybody had an  interest in inflating this bubble  because it it was really it was like  apple pie everybody loved this from left  to right it's home ownership its  property rights something for the right  to make sure that people own something  for themselves and they become become  citizens and in that regard for the left  it was to oppose discrimination to make  sure that even the poorest of the poor  could own a house of their own and they  were involved all of them and now  they're trying to blame one another now  they're looking at just the parts of  them the pieces of the puzzle where the  other  site made mistakes and then they tried  to pretend that they did not do this  let's take this out of it further so we  have an American housing bubble we have  a Fed that is that is creating  incredibly cheap money how is this  spreading out globally you have a  chapter in the book or you have a  section in the book where you talk about  the collapse for instance of Iceland  which is a colossal collapse and this is  rocking markets across across the globe  how is that happening yeah well you can  say as someone who was involved in this  and American banks and lenders were they  did bad things and it's strange that  they really could create all these  securities based on these very risky  mortgages but even stranger is the fact  that others bought this and took on  these risks and that was the part that  really made this global the fact that  banks and governments all around the  world they bought into and they wanted a  piece of the action so they bought  mortgage-backed securities and that  means that everybody was so incredibly  exposed to this bubble when it began to  deflate the final chapter in your book  is called tomorrow capitalism with the  question mark what is what does this  mean for for capitalism for free markets  for less a fair economics is the  foundations are being shaken right now  and we have people that are nominally  right-of-center saying hey this is the  markets fault  are we seeing like an enormous crisis in  capitalism and tell us a little bit  about the last chapter in the book well  yeah yeah we are witnessing a a huge  crisis of capitalism and especially when  it comes to our our vision of what  capitalism is about because we do know  that people on the market have made  colossal mistakes banks and investment  banks have made bizarre mistakes that  they should have been able to foresee so  obviously it's easy to blame them for  what happened and I certainly I'm one of  those who who blame them  so therefore we see a backlash all over  the world the problem is that people  don't really realize that the  regulator's the central banks and the  politicians they were inflating the  bubble just as eagerly they only had a  bigger pump so they could do it even  more dramatically and and that's  something that we're missing now when  people think that the response is to  bail out all the misguided investments  to regulate financial markets even more  and and that's building up problems for  the future one of the biggest problems I  think is that we're now giving all the  actors on the financial markets an idea  that now they definitely got a  government guarantee in the future  because the government will always step  in and bail them out if there's a  mistake if there's a problem somewhere  and then we'll see worse risks Johan  Norberg author of a financial Fiasco  which is at now and can be purchased  online at amazon.com and fine bookstores  everywhere thanks for joining us thank  you for reason TV I'm Michael Moynihan

